stem_md:
Under the rules of the "Sea battle" game (no two ships may have a common point), can the following sets of rectangular ships be arranged on a  $ 10 \times 10$  square board:
 $ (a)$  two ships  $ 1 \times 4$ ,  $ 4$  ships  $ 1 \times 3$ ,  $ 6$  ships  $ 1 \times 2$ , and  $ 8$  ships  $ 1 \times 1$ ;
 $ (b)$  two ships  $ 1 \times 4$ ,  $ 4$  ships  $ 1 \times 3$ ,  $ 6$  ships  $ 1 \times 2$ ,  $ 6$  ships  $ 1 \times 1$ , and  $ 1$  ship  $ 2 \times 2$ ;
 $ (c)$  two ships  $ 1 \times 4$ ,  $ 4$  ships  $ 1 \times 3$ ,  $ 6$  ships  $ 1 \times 2$ ,  $ 4$  ships  $ 1 \times 1$ , and  $ 2$  ships  $ 2 \times 2$ ?

Find all monotone (not necessarily strictly) functions  $ f: \mathbb{R}^{\plus{}}_0\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$  such that:
 $ f(x\plus{}y)\minus{}f(x)\minus{}f(y)\equal{}f(xy\plus{}1)\minus{}f(xy)\minus{}f(1) \forall x,y \ge 0$ ;
 $ f(3)\plus{}3f(1)\equal{}3f(2)\plus{}f(0).$

candidates:
Like I said before, take any function  $a: [0, 1) \to R$ , and any  $b \in \mathbb{R}$ . Then take  $f$  such that:  $$ f(x)=\begin{cases} x a(\{\log_{n+1}(|\ln(x)|) \}) &\mbox{if } x \in \mathbb{R}^+ \setminus \{1\}
b & \mbox{if } x = 1 \end{cases} $$  Any such function satisfies  $f(x^{n+1})=x^nf(x)$  for all  $x>0$ . Therefore they all satisfy  $f(x^{n+1})+f(y^{n+1})=x^nf(x)+y^nf(y)$  for all  $x, y > 0$ .

Given this, you're still convinced that  $f$  is linear?

The only answer is  $p=3$ .

Fix  $p$  and suppose such a  $g$  exists. First, observe that  $g$  must be a primitive root modulo  $p$  as all of the  $k^2$  in  $A$  are distinct and  $1 \notin A$ . Furthermore,  $p \equiv 3 \pmod{4}$ , else  $0 \in B$ . Let  $P_A$  and  $P_B$  denote the product of the elements of  $A$  and  $B$  respectively. Working modulo  $p$  and using the [fact](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h1984844p13808756) that  $P_A^2 \equiv 4$ , we have 
\begin{align*}
4 \equiv P^2_A & \equiv P^2_B 
&\equiv \left(g^{\tfrac{p-1}{2}}\right)^{\tfrac{p+1}{2}} 
&\equiv \left(-1\right)^{\tfrac{p+1}{2}}
&\equiv 1,
\end{align*}
so  $p = 3$ . It's trivial to see that  $g = 2$  works, so this is the only solution.

