Rewrite as   $$ \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{3}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{7}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{13}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{21}\right) $$    $$ =\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{1+1\cdot 2}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{1+2\cdot 3}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{1+3\cdot 4}\right) + \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{1}{1+4\cdot 5}\right) $$  From  $\tan^{-1}\left( \frac{x-y}{1+xy}\right) = \tan^{-1} x - \tan^{-1} y$ , we can rewrite this as   $$ (\tan^{-1}2-\tan^{-1}1)+(\tan^{-1}3-\tan^{-1}2)+(\tan^{-1}4-\tan^{-1}3)+(\tan^{-1}5-\tan^{-1}4) $$  Then, to find  $10\cot(\tan^{-1} 5 - \tan^{-1} 1)$  consider the following diagram. 

[asy]
size(10cm); // Increased size for clarity

// Points
pair A = (0,0);
pair B = (2,0);
pair P = (2,2);
pair C = (2,10);

// Draw figure
draw(A--B--P--C--A--P);

// Side labels
label(" $1$ ", midpoint(A--B), S);         // AB
label(" $1$ ", midpoint(B--P), E);         // PB
label(" $4$ ", midpoint(B--C), E);         // BC
label(" $\sqrt{26}$ ", midpoint(C--A), NW); // AC
label(" $\sqrt{2}$ ", midpoint(A--P) + 0.4*dir(135)); // PA label with offset

// Point labels
dot(" $A$ ", A, SW);
dot(" $B$ ", B, SE);
dot(" $P$ ", P, NE);
dot(" $C$ ", C, NE);

// Angle label
label(" $\theta$ ", A + 0.6*dir(65));
[/asy]

Since  $\theta=\tan^{-1}5-\tan^{-1}1$ , we want to find  $10\cot\theta$ .

By the law of cosines,  $4^2=2+26-2\sqrt{2\cdot 26}\cos \theta$ , so  $\cos\theta = \frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}$  and  $\sin\theta = \sqrt{1-\cos^2\theta}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}$ . Then,  $\cot\theta = \frac{\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}=\frac{3/\sqrt{13}}{2/\sqrt{13}}=\frac{3}{2}$  and  $10\cot\theta = 10\cdot \frac{3}{2} = \boxed{15}$ .

