The interior angle of  $n$ -gon is  $\alpha_n=\frac{(n-2)\cdot 180^\circ}{n}$ , and that of a  $s$ -gon is  $\alpha_s=\frac{(s-2)\cdot 180^\circ}{s}$ . Hence

\begin{eqnarray*}\frac{(n-2)s}{n(s-2)}={59\over 58} &\iff& 58ns-116s=59ns-118n &\iff& -ns-116s=-118n &\iff& s=\frac{118n}{n+116} &\iff& s=\frac{118n+118\cdot 116-118\cdot 116}{n+116} &\iff& s=118-\frac{118\cdot 116}{n+116}\end{eqnarray*}

For  $s$  to be maximal, the expression  $\frac{118\cdot 116}{n+116}\end{eqnarray*}$  must be minimal, which in turn means that  $n+116$  must be maximal while in the same time it must divide  $118\cdot 116.$  Obviously,  $n+116$  must be equal to  $118\cdot 116$ , which gives  $s_{max}=117.$

