The given equation is equivalent to  $\frac{1 + \sin x}{\cos x} = \frac{22}{7}$  and we are asked to find  $\frac{1 + \cos x}{\sin x}.$  It turns out that an algebraic trick allows us to easily compute the answer: Note that 
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{1 + \sin x}{\cos x}\right)\left(\frac{1 + \cos x}{\sin x}\right) &= \frac{\sin x \cos x + \sin x + \cos x + 1}{\sin x \cos x} 
&= 1 + \frac{\sin x + \cos x + \sin^2 x + \cos^2 x}{\sin x \cos x} 
&= 1 + \frac{\sin x \left(1 + \sin x\right) + \cos x \left(1 + \cos x\right)}{\sin x \cos x} 
&= 1 + \frac{1 + \sin x}{\cos x} + \frac{1 + \cos x}{\sin x}.
\end{align*} Thus, we have reduced the product of two quantities into their sum! Since we know that  $\frac{1 + \sin x}{\cos x} = \frac{22}{7}$ , our equation rewrites as \[\frac{22}{7}t = 1 + \frac{22}{7} + t\] where  $t = \frac{1 + \cos x}{\sin x}$  is what we are trying to compute. It is then easy to find that  $t = \frac{29}{15}.$  This gives us an answer of  $29 + 15 = \boxed{44}.$

