If we find how much is covered in the other two regions, we can take the complement and divide by 2 to find the area of the region bounded by the two unequal segments. Notice that the area of regions AEC and DEB is equal to sector(DOB) + sector(AOC) + area(DOBE) - area(AECO). We can find this. First, we must figure out the angle of each of the sectors. Notice that triangle AOE has sides 18,30,42. Applying Law of Cosines, we find that  $\cos(\angle AOE) = \frac{11}{14}$ . So the angle of sector AOC is  $2\cdot \arccos(\frac{11}{14}).$  To find the angle of DOB, we can again apply Law of Cosines on triangle DOE. This has sides 42, 18, 48. We get  $\cos(\angle DOE) = -\frac 17$ . So the angle measure of  $DOB$  is  $360-2 \cdot \arccos( - \frac 17)$ . Adding these two sector angles, we get  $360 + 2(\arccos (\frac{11}{14}) - \arccos(-\frac 17)).$  Now let  $x = \arccos (\frac{11}{14}) - \arccos(-\frac 17)$ . We can take the cosine of both sides to get  $\cos(x) = \cos(\arccos (\frac{11}{14}) - \arccos(-\frac 17)).$  Using the subtraction formula, we get  $\cos(x) = -\frac{11}{14} \cdot  \frac{1}{7} + \frac{5\sqrt3}{14} \cdot \frac{4\sqrt3}{7} = \frac{1}{2}$ . Then  $x= \pm 60$  degrees. But it has to be negative, since if it's positive, the sum of the angles is more than  $360$ . Then we get  $360 - 2(60) = 240$ . Since it covers 240 degrees, this is  $\frac 23 \cdot 42^2 \cdot \pi = 1176 \pi$ .

