Reviving this thread, but it's just such a nice question so that's that

<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>\begin{align*}
\log(\sin x) + \log(\cos x) &= -1 
\Rightarrow \log(\sin x \cos x) &= -1 
\Rightarrow \sin x \cos x &= 10^{-1} = \frac{1}{10}
\end{align*}

Also,
\[
\log_{10}(\sin x + \cos x) = \frac{1}{2} (\log_{10} n - 1)
\]
\begin{align*}
\Rightarrow \log_{10} \left( \frac{\sin x + \cos x}{\sqrt{n}} \right) &= -\frac{1}{2} 
\Rightarrow \frac{\sin x + \cos x}{\sqrt{n}} &= 10^{-1/2} 
\Rightarrow \sin x + \cos x &= \sqrt{n} \cdot 10^{-1/2} 
\Rightarrow \sin x + \cos x &= \sqrt{n} \cdot \left( \frac{1}{10} \right)^{1/2}
\end{align*}

By substitution,
\begin{align*}
\sin x + \cos x &= \sqrt{n} \cdot (\sin x \cos x)^{1/2}
\end{align*}

Squaring both sides,
\begin{align*}
(\sin x + \cos x)^2 &= n \cdot \sin x \cos x 
1 + 2 \sin x \cos x &= n \cdot \sin x \cos x 
\Rightarrow n &= \frac{1 + 2 \sin x \cos x}{\sin x \cos x}
\end{align*}

Substituting  $\sin x \cos x = \frac{1}{10}$ ,
\begin{align*}
n &= \frac{1 + 2 \cdot \frac{1}{10}}{\frac{1}{10}} = \frac{6}{5} \cdot 10 = 12
\end{align*}

\[
\boxed{n = 12}
\]</details>

